Track 2: Baggage-Mistress Vane
- Story & Context: Every morning before the morning bell, Baggage-Mistress Vane dusted the rain-streaked chalkboards at the pier where the rail siding terminated into the canal basin, quietly hiding bootleg cassette tapes inside crates of damp river reed. She admitted to the market elders that she had intentionally misrouted an entire freight manifest just to buy an extra hour of silence on the dock with her cranked baritone Telecaster and a water-damaged analog delay. The depot master confronted her with a wrench to cut her generator cables, but he was stopped dead when four market grandmothers raised their French horns and blasted a crushing, low-register brass wall that rattled the buttons off his uniform. In the airy, ringing silence left behind by the decaying feedback, Vane wiped grease from her collar and whispered, "I filed your timetable under river silt."
