Track 3: Locomotives Drown in Kerosene
- Story & Context: Long after the southern trestle collapsed into the black river water, divers salvaging brass fittings from the sunken freight cars insisted that the submerged iron boilers still resonated with the morning tide. Produce vendors who anchored their skiffs over the wreck confirmed that whenever the morning cold grew sharp enough to freeze the diesel scum, a ghostly, fuzzed-out French horn riff could be felt vibrating through the timber hulls. The legend hardened into scene scripture when an archivist lowered a hydrophone into the canal and captured a roaring swell of distorted lap steel and airy vocal moans vibrating deep within the silted engine room. Gathering around the floating market stoves as the tape played back, the old women smiled and spoke the myth aloud: "What the water derailed, the brass will carry home."
